---
description: Find, assess, and resolve worthwhile duplicate code
---

Treat `$ARGUMENTS` as the requested scope and options, not a Beads scope. At invocation, establish whether to scan the requested target(s) or focus on a Git diff. In target mode, resolve the requested path(s) and scan only those; use the repository root only when the whole project is explicitly requested. In diff mode, use an actual Git diff with the user-specified base and selection, including committed branch changes and/or staged or unstaged changes only as chosen by the user. Ask only for missing or ambiguous inputs; never silently choose a base or equate a Git diff with Git status.

At invocation, provide an explicit choice to include or exclude tests; if the user has not specified it, ask before scanning. This gate is language-agnostic. When tests are included, favor readable, self-contained test stories over extracting helpers solely to reduce duplication. When tests are excluded, identify test files and directories conservatively using repository conventions and exclude them from detection where practical, without broadly suppressing production code. If using `jscpd --ignore`, preserve every existing managed ignore entry because this option replaces the config's ignore list; add only narrow test exclusions. Do not assume an `--include-tests` option changes clone detection. When tests are excluded, discard any remaining findings in test code during assessment, including inline test modules; for mixed production/test files, ignore only test-code locations, not production code. When tests are included, test-code findings remain eligible for assessment, with readability favored over extracting helpers solely to deduplicate.

Run local jscpd with the managed config and compact reporter on the resolved target or chosen comparison corpus:

```sh
jscpd --config "$HOME/.config/opencode/jscpd.json" --reporters ai --no-tips --fail-on-empty -- "<resolved-target-or-corpus>"
```

Replace the placeholder with the resolved path or corpus; for multiple paths, pass each as a separate quoted argument. In diff mode, use the actual selected diff to identify changed files and changed-line ranges, and retain only candidates with a duplicate location intersecting those ranges. jscpd does not filter clones by changed lines, so assess its reported locations against the diff. To find duplication between changed and unchanged project code, include an appropriate wider comparison corpus containing both; scanning only changed files cannot find unchanged-file matches. Report the actual corpus, exclusions and limitations, and never claim full-project coverage when only changed files were scanned. Do not use `jscpd --changed` (it follows Git status and may write `.jscpd-baseline.json`) or `--changed-only` (it misses unchanged-file matches); avoid unexpected baseline or worktree side effects. Keep local-only detection: do not substitute project configuration, use MCP or remote analysis services, or enable embeddings or downloads.

Read reported source fragments and nearby context to assess whether each finding is genuinely equivalent and worth simplifying. Report clone locations, actual scope and material limitations, including excluded code, unavailable cross-target matches, scan failures, empty analysis or truncated output. Do not treat incomplete results as a complete scan.

For each candidate, assign a 1–3 value/feasibility score: 3 means a clear, worthwhile change; 2 means a genuine unresolved trade-off that needs user or architectural judgment; 1 means leave it alone, and ignore it for execution. Explain the score briefly. Preserve intentional repetition and never turn superficial similarity into an abstraction.

Omit score-1 findings from approval. Before asking, present the number of score-3 findings in a brief paragraph; retain their individual evidence and details for precise execution briefs. If there are score-2 findings, show them in a table with columns `Index`, `Affected code`, `Trade-off`, and `Recommendation`; for each finding, give concrete individual file paths and line numbers or ranges for every duplicate location. Do not compress paths into brace notation without individual line references. Describe the genuine trade-off requiring judgment and include a recommendation where useful. Do not show a score-1 finding, and omit the table when there are no score-2 findings.

If there are no score-2 or score-3 findings, report that nothing worthwhile is approved and stop without asking or editing. Otherwise, use the question tool exactly once for one questionnaire containing all applicable questions—never use sequential questionnaires: (1) yes/no approval of all score-3 findings, when any exist; (2) one concise score-2 question using the question tool's built-in free-form answer, asking for `all`, `none`, or selected table indices (for example, `1, 2, 5`), when any exist; and (3) waves or one batch, with a recommendation, only when the number and relationships of findings make that choice meaningful. Do not create a separate option for each score-2 finding. If the index answer is ambiguous, approve no score-2 finding rather than guessing. A single-batch choice still requires safe ordering for dependencies and deliberate handling of overlapping code. Respect each answer independently, including declining all score-3 findings while selecting score-2 findings. Execute only the approved findings; if none are approved, stop without editing.

For approved work, create self-contained, score-free execution briefs identifying the concrete duplication, affected files/code, intended outcome, and relevant constraints; do not mention score categories or depend on the assessment context. If waves were selected and warranted, group the briefs into manageable waves accounting for overlap and dependencies. Implement only within the requested target, then summarize changes and remaining findings. Never execute score-1 findings or unselected score-2 findings.
